# #####################################################################################################################
#
#   FAST Studio settings file  (.env)
#
# #####################################################################################################################
#
#   DO I NEED THIS?
#   Not to try FAST Studio: start.bat works without it. Use it when you want to:
#     - send real emails (invitations, password links, alerts)          -> section 1
#     - run on your own domain, with HTTPS                              -> section 2
#     - keep video links working across restarts (outside pilot mode)   -> section 3
#     - choose the first administrator of a pilot yourself              -> section 4
#     - use your own PostgreSQL database server                         -> section 5
#
#   HOW TO SET IT UP (3 steps)
#     1. Make a copy of this file called ".env" in the same folder (next to start.bat):
#          Windows (Command Prompt):  copy .env.example .env
#          Windows (PowerShell):      Copy-Item .env.example .env
#          macOS / Linux:             cp .env.example .env
#     2. Open ".env" in any text editor (Notepad is fine). Every setting below is switched OFF: its line starts
#        with "#". To switch one ON, delete the "#" (and the space after it) at the start of the line and put in
#        your value, the same way as the example.
#     3. Save the file and restart FAST Studio: stop.bat, then start.bat (or start.bat -Pilot).
#
#   RULES (so it works first time)
#     - One setting per line: NAME=value. No spaces around "=".
#     - If the value contains spaces, put it in double quotes:  NAME="value with spaces"
#     - Don't need a setting? Leave its "#" in place. Never leave a line as "NAME=" with nothing after it.
#     - .env holds passwords: never email it or put it in git (git already ignores it).
#     - In pilot mode the file data-pilot\pilot.env is read AFTER this one; if both set the same NAME, pilot.env wins.
#
#   ALL SETTINGS with their defaults: backend/src/main/resources/application.yaml
#
# #####################################################################################################################



# =====================================================================================================================
#   1. SEND REAL EMAILS
# =====================================================================================================================
#   Without this, FAST Studio still "sends" every email, but saves it as a file (data\mail-outbox\*.eml) and lists
#   it on the Email page instead of delivering it. Perfect for trying things out.
#
#   STEP A - Pick ONE email service and get its sign-in details:
#
#     Just testing (nothing reaches real people) - Mailtrap Email Testing, free
#        1. Sign up: https://mailtrap.io/   Guide: https://help.mailtrap.io/article/109-getting-started-with-mailtrap-email-testing
#        2. Open your test inbox -> "SMTP Settings": copy Host, Port, Username, Password.
#        3. Your value:  smtp://USERNAME:PASSWORD@HOST:PORT     e.g. smtp://1a2b3c4d5e6f7a:9z8y7x6w5v4u3t@sandbox.smtp.mailtrap.io:2525
#
#     SendGrid
#        1. Create an API key with "Mail Send" permission: https://app.sendgrid.com/settings/api_keys
#        2. Verify the sender address you will send from: https://www.twilio.com/docs/sendgrid/ui/sending-email/sender-verification
#        3. Your value (the user name is literally the word apikey):  smtps://apikey:YOUR_API_KEY@smtp.sendgrid.net:465
#        Help: https://www.twilio.com/docs/sendgrid/for-developers/sending-email/integrating-with-the-smtp-api
#
#     Amazon SES
#        1. Create SMTP credentials (these are NOT your AWS login): https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ses/latest/dg/smtp-credentials.html
#        2. Find your region's host name: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ses/latest/dg/smtp-connect.html
#        3. Verify your sending domain or address: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ses/latest/dg/creating-identities.html
#        4. Your value:  smtps://SMTP_USERNAME:SMTP_PASSWORD@email-smtp.REGION.amazonaws.com:465
#
#     Google Workspace / Gmail
#        1. Turn on 2-Step Verification, then create an "App password": https://support.google.com/accounts/answer/185833
#        2. Your value:  smtps://you%40yourcompany.com:APP_PASSWORD@smtp.gmail.com:465
#        Help: https://support.google.com/a/answer/176600
#
#     Microsoft 365 / Outlook
#        1. Your Microsoft 365 admin must allow "Authenticated SMTP" for the mailbox:
#           https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/exchange/clients-and-mobile-in-exchange-online/authenticated-client-smtp-submission
#        2. Your value:  smtp://you%40yourcompany.com:PASSWORD@smtp.office365.com:587
#
#   STEP B - Special characters: inside the value, write "@" in a user name as %40. If the password contains any of
#     these characters, replace them:  @ -> %40   # -> %23   / -> %2F   : -> %3A   ? -> %3F   % -> %25   space -> %20
#     Or let the computer do it (Node.js is installed with FAST Studio):
#        node -e "console.log(encodeURIComponent('your password here'))"
#     More: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Percent-encoding
#
#   STEP C - Put your value below, choose the sender, restart, and test: sign in as platform administrator ->
#     Email -> "Send a test email". The Email page shows "Emails are delivered" when it worked.
#   Technical reference for the value format (it is handed to nodemailer as SMTP_URL): https://nodemailer.com/smtp
#
# FASTSTUDIO_SMTP_URL=smtps://apikey:YOUR_API_KEY@smtp.sendgrid.net:465
#
#   Sender name and address shown on every email. Use an address your email service lets you send from
#   (the verified sender/domain from step A). Keep the double quotes.
# FASTSTUDIO_MAIL_FROM="FAST Studio <no-reply@yourcompany.com>"



# =====================================================================================================================
#   2. YOUR OWN ADDRESS (DOMAIN) AND HTTPS
# =====================================================================================================================
#   FASTSTUDIO_APP_URL - the address people type in the browser to open FAST Studio.
#     Need it? Yes, as soon as people use anything other than http://localhost:8080 (another computer, a domain).
#     Links inside emails start with it, so a wrong value means broken links in invitations and password emails.
#     Where to get it: it is your own choice: the domain or server address you give FAST Studio. No "/" at the end.
# FASTSTUDIO_APP_URL=https://studio.yourcompany.com
#
#   FASTSTUDIO_SECURE_COOKIES - set to true when that address starts with https://
#     Need it? Yes with HTTPS, so sign-in cookies only travel encrypted. Leave it off for plain http://localhost.
#     How to get HTTPS: put a web server with automatic certificates in front of FAST Studio, e.g. Caddy:
#        https://caddyserver.com/docs/quick-starts/reverse-proxy
#        caddy reverse-proxy --from studio.yourcompany.com --to localhost:8080
# FASTSTUDIO_SECURE_COOKIES=true



# =====================================================================================================================
#   3. VIDEO LINK SIGNING KEY
# =====================================================================================================================
#   FASTSTUDIO_MEDIA_SECRET - a secret key that signs the addresses of video pieces, so nobody can guess or reuse them.
#     Need it?  Pilot mode: NO, it is created for you in data-pilot\pilot.env.
#               Demo mode: optional. Without it a new key is made at every start, so video links opened before a
#               restart stop working (players simply reload).
#               Several servers: YES, and exactly the same value on all of them.
#     Where to get it: make one yourself (64 random characters). Run this and paste the result:
#        node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
#     Keep it secret, and keep it the same: changing it invalidates links that are currently open.
# FASTSTUDIO_MEDIA_SECRET=paste-the-64-characters-here
#
#   Viewers are served by the Cloudflare Worker in worker/ (live playlists, ad stitching, video from R2).
#   FASTSTUDIO_EDGE_SECRET - shared between this server and the Worker: authorises uploads and signs viewer sessions.
#     Need it?  Locally: NO, start.bat generates one into data\runtime\edge.env and worker\.dev.vars.
#               Deployed Worker: YES, the same value here and as the Worker secret (wrangler secret put EDGE_SECRET).
#   FASTSTUDIO_EDGE_URL - where this server reaches the Worker (default http://127.0.0.1:8787).
#   FASTSTUDIO_EDGE_PUBLIC_URL - the Worker's address as viewers see it, e.g. https://play.yourcompany.com
# FASTSTUDIO_EDGE_SECRET=paste-64-random-characters-here
# FASTSTUDIO_EDGE_URL=https://play.yourcompany.com
# FASTSTUDIO_EDGE_PUBLIC_URL=https://play.yourcompany.com
#
#   FASTSTUDIO_SECRETS_KEY - encrypts the video library (Mux) credentials the platform administrator stores per company.
#     Need it?  Optional: without it a key is generated once into data\keys\secrets.key (keep that file in backups).
#               Several servers: YES, the same value on all of them. Changing it means re-entering stored credentials.
# FASTSTUDIO_SECRETS_KEY=paste-64-random-characters-here



# =====================================================================================================================
#   4. FIRST ADMINISTRATOR OF A PILOT
# =====================================================================================================================
#   Used once, when a pilot starts for the first time and no platform administrator exists yet.
#     Need it? Only if you want to choose this login yourself. Otherwise the pilot creates admin@pilot.local with a
#     random temporary password and saves it in data-pilot\FIRST-SIGN-IN.txt.
#     Where to get it: your own work email, and any temporary password of at least 10 characters that doesn't
#     contain your email name. You must choose a new password at the first sign-in anyway.
#     Set them BEFORE the first "start.bat -Pilot" (they are copied into data-pilot\pilot.env then).
# FASTSTUDIO_ADMIN_EMAIL=you@yourcompany.com
# FASTSTUDIO_ADMIN_PASSWORD=Temporary-Strong-Pass-1



# =====================================================================================================================
#   5. YOUR OWN DATABASE SERVER (PostgreSQL)
# =====================================================================================================================
#   Need it? Only for production on a managed database. By default FAST Studio runs its own built-in PostgreSQL:
#   nothing to install, data in the data folder.
#   Requirements: PostgreSQL 14 or newer, an empty database, and a user allowed to create tables (FAST Studio
#   creates them on its first start).
#   Where to get the values: your database provider's "connection details" page, for example:
#     Amazon RDS    https://aws.amazon.com/rds/postgresql/
#     Azure         https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/postgresql/
#     Google Cloud  https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres
#   Write the address as  jdbc:postgresql://HOST:PORT/DATABASE   (add ?sslmode=require if the provider asks for SSL).
#   Format reference: https://jdbc.postgresql.org/documentation/use/
# DB_URL=jdbc:postgresql://your-db-host.example.com:5432/faststudio?sslmode=require
# DB_USER=faststudio
# DB_PASSWORD=the-database-password
#   (start.bat -Docker runs a database in Docker instead and manages its own password in data\runtime\docker.env.)



# =====================================================================================================================
#   6. RARELY NEEDED
# =====================================================================================================================
#   Store all data (database, videos, emails, keys) in another folder or disk. Default: the "data" folder
#   (pilot: "data-pilot"). Use forward slashes.
# FASTSTUDIO_DATA_DIR=D:/faststudio-data
#
#   How people stay signed in: jwt (default, recommended) or session (old method, only if support asks you).
# FASTSTUDIO_AUTH_MODE=jwt
#
#   Requests per minute one partner organization may make to the partner API (default 120). Raise it for a big partner.
# FASTSTUDIO_PARTNERS_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=300
#
#   Days before a new sign-in signing key is made (default 90).
# FASTSTUDIO_AUTH_KEY_ROTATION_DAYS=90
#
#   Only if videos fail with "ffmpeg not found": the full path of ffmpeg. It normally comes with
#   "npm install" in the frontend folder; otherwise download it from https://ffmpeg.org/download.html
# FASTSTUDIO_FFMPEG=C:/tools/ffmpeg/bin/ffmpeg.exe
#
#   Only if emails fail with a Node.js error: the full path of node. Find it with:  where node  (Windows)
#   or  which node  (macOS/Linux). Node.js itself: https://nodejs.org/
# FASTSTUDIO_MAIL_NODE=C:/Program Files/nodejs/node.exe
#
#   Any other setting in application.yaml works the same way: write its name in capitals, with dots and dashes
#   as underscores. Example: faststudio.partners.rate-limit-per-minute -> FASTSTUDIO_PARTNERS_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE
